# name: test/sql/fallible_v2.test
# description: class 13 -- throwing scalar functions are declared fallible; the type prefix is what discriminates
# group: [sql]

require duck_block_utils

# On DuckDB v2.0 an undeclared throw of an EXECUTION-ERROR type is rewritten as
#   INTERNAL Error: Scalar function "x" threw an execution error, but the function is not marked as fallible ... Error: <raw message>
# which still CONTAINS the raw message, so a bare-substring `statement error` passes on
# both the correct and the mis-declared form; expecting the exception's TYPE PREFIX
# immediately before the message is what distinguishes them.
#
# MEASURED on v2.0-cyanoptera (2026-09-13, throwaway/class13-observe): the rewrite is
# gated by Exception::IsExecutionError(type) in ThrowNonFallibleFunctionError
# (src/function/scalar_function.cpp), and IOException is NOT an execution error, so with
# write_pandoc_ast's SetFallible() removed the 2.0 leg still printed exactly
#   IO Error: Could not open file for writing: /nonexistent-dir/x.json
# The two IO cases below therefore pin the message and prefix but CANNOT discriminate the
# fallible declaration. The discriminating case is an InvalidInputException, the depth cap
# in test/sql/pandoc_recursion_cap.test:28-32 (Invalid Input Error: ...), whose mis-declared
# form is being observed on a third throwaway run. On v1.5.5 the flag is consulted by the
# planner (CanThrow) and not enforced, so every case passes either way there.
#
# write_pandoc_ast / read_pandoc_ast are deprecated in favour of panduck's own readers;
# these two cases move there with them.

statement error
SELECT write_pandoc_ast('/nonexistent-dir/x.json', [duck_block_heading('H', 1)]);
----
IO Error: Could not open file for writing

statement error
SELECT read_pandoc_ast('/nonexistent-dir/none.json');
----
IO Error: Could not open file

# CheckPandocDepth's type-prefix discrimination ("Invalid Input Error: ...") is already
# asserted at test/sql/pandoc_recursion_cap.test:28-32 (same construction, same expected
# string); not repeated here.
